{"kind": "chat_performance", "workload": "python", "repeat": 1, "prompt": "Write a Python function that parses timestamped log lines, groups errors by hour, and returns the five most frequent messages. Explain edge cases and include tests.", "input_ids": [248045, 846, 198, 7734, 264, 12654, 709, 421, 68434, 11112, 290, 1433, 4965, 11, 5038, 5787, 539, 6258, 11, 321, 4523, 279, 4097, 1379, 20125, 6397, 13, 79091, 6607, 4886, 321, 2830, 6813, 13, 248046, 198, 248045, 74455, 198, 248068, 271, 248069, 271], "prompt_tokens": 43, "output_tokens": 256, "answer": "```python\nimport re\nfrom collections import defaultdict, Counter\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Optional\n\n\ndef parse_log_line(line: str) -> Optional[Tuple[datetime, str]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parse a single log line and return (timestamp, message) if valid.\n    \n    Expected format: [YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS] message\n    Example: [2023-10-05 14:30:45] ERROR: Connection timeout\n    \n    Returns None if the line cannot be parsed.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not line or not line.strip():\n        return None\n    \n    # Match timestamp in brackets followed by the message\n    pattern = r'^\\[(\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2} \\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2})\\]\\s*(.*)$'\n    match = re.match(pattern, line.strip())\n    \n    if not match:\n        return None\n    \n    timestamp_str = match.group(1)\n    message = match.group(2).strip()\n    \n    try:\n        timestamp = datetime.strptime(timestamp_str, '%Y-%m", "input_sha256": "ed3cc12c1c494580eaed3e90d72603925183e04858c5e3cb4544467f5508d4ce", "output_sha256": "acd59b9b42f28dc06ad6ba2af44ff21fb16a8686d769f3ae11f0eb08f7b76179", "ttft_seconds": 1.309179700911045, "elapsed_seconds": 15.319796487689018, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 43, "prompt_ms": 1255.7, "prompt_per_token_ms": 29.202, "prompt_per_second": 34.2, "predicted_n": 256, "predicted_ms": 14062.4, "predicted_per_token_ms": 54.931, "predicted_per_second": 18.2, "draft_n": 0, "draft_n_accepted": 0}, "engine": {"generated": 256, "prompt_tokens": 43, "prompt_ms": 1255.7, "decode_ms": 14062.4, "finish": "length", "drafts_accepted": 0, "drafts_offered": 0, "reused": 0, "hits": 114580, "lookups": 122880, "ram_blobs": 8300, "file_blobs": 0, "file_mb": 0.0, "prompt_read": 43, "offloaded": 0, "chunk": 0, "experts_streamed": 0}, "gpus": 2, "workers": 8, "profile": false, "aux_cpus": "off", "mode": "layer", "shared_stream": "default"}
{"kind": "chat_performance", "workload": "explanation", "repeat": 1, "prompt": "Explain how hash tables handle collisions, resizing, and adversarial input. Compare separate chaining with open addressing and give practical examples.", "input_ids": [248045, 846, 198, 814, 20139, 1204, 5010, 12269, 3579, 45776, 11, 83264, 11, 321, 27483, 41306, 1879, 13, 23024, 8387, 71159, 440, 1724, 26153, 321, 2873, 14542, 9989, 13, 248046, 198, 248045, 74455, 198, 248068, 271, 248069, 271], "prompt_tokens": 38, "output_tokens": 256, "answer": "Hash tables are fundamental data structures that provide average-case $O(1)$ time complexity for insertions, deletions, and lookups. However, achieving this performance requires robust mechanisms to handle **collisions**, **resizing**, and **adversarial inputs**.\n\nBelow is a detailed explanation of these mechanisms, followed by a comparison of collision resolution strategies and practical examples.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Handling Collisions\n\nA collision occurs when two distinct keys hash to the same index in the underlying array. Since the hash function maps a large key space to a smaller array index space, collisions are inevitable (Pigeonhole Principle).\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining\nIn separate chaining, each bucket in the hash table array points to a linked list (or another dynamic structure) of entries that hash to that index.\n\n- **Mechanism**: When a collision occurs, the new entry is appended to the list at that bucket.\n- **Pros**:\n  - Simple to implement.\n  - The table never becomes \"full\" in the sense that insertion is always possible (memory permitting).\n  - Deletion is straightforward.\n- **Cons**:\n  - Poor cache locality due to pointer chasing.\n  - Performance degrades to $O", "input_sha256": "2d4cd832e51e7b84accb51b33cdbe69630c5b76dc17c0986f9fdaf9d6248b23b", "output_sha256": "b5104dce7ce3f73a84e4d2e72848e8dac261c573f244e73ec16291e85594ac3c", "ttft_seconds": 1.1916485130786896, "elapsed_seconds": 14.861122831702232, "timin